preConditionHook:
// GRIMES'S NAMED FAILED BREAKOUTS TEMPLATE (G06-60), carried as the book
// states it and with its parts INLINED, as the corpus does for a named setup.
// A breakout happened in the last maxBarsSinceBreakout bars, it showed WEAK
// range and tick count -- "good breakouts should have strong momentum, volume
// and interest beyond the level; if this does not happen, it is more likely
// that the breakout will fail" (G06-56) -- and THIS bar is a strong
// countertrend reaction (G06-57). Take the reversal.
//
// SIDES: a failed UPSIDE breakout is the SHORT and a failed DOWNSIDE breakout
// the LONG, which is why the long condition pairs weakDn with a strong BULL
// reaction bar.
//
// THE SEQUENCE IS THE RULE, and it is what no pane-AND reaches: the range and
// tick tests are evaluated AT THE BREAKOUT BAR, a bar found by a backward scan,
// while the body test is evaluated on THIS bar. Two entry panes both evaluate
// on the same bar, so BreakoutBarRangeAndVolumeExpansion negated in a pane
// would judge bar 0, not bar kk. That ORDERING CONSTRAINT, not an OR, is this
// template's reason to exist.
//
// NOT FailedBreakoutReversal, which needs a closing price back through the
// broken window extreme and says nothing about the quality of the breakout bar or the
// strength of the reaction; this never reads the level again after the break.
// Not FailedBreakoutBeyondExtreme or MarginalBreakoutThenReverse, the
// poke-and-reclaim forms. BreakoutPullbackWindow supplies the backward-scan
// shape this reuses.
//
// THE BOOK'S ALTERNATIVE RESOLUTION -- "a plain failure test at the prior high
// or low" (G06-01) -- is DELIBERATELY NOT FOLDED IN: it is
// FailedBreakoutReversal AND BodyExceedsAverageBarRange placed alongside, per
// merge decision 13.
//
// THE LEVEL IS PER-CANDIDATE and open-coded, FailedBreakoutReversal's
// construction: for a candidate breakout bar kk the level is the extreme of
// bars kk+1 .. kk+breakoutLookback, which in EasyLanguage would be the highest
// high of that window read at offset kk + 1 -- a history reference on a
// function call at a VARIABLE offset. Extremes(), which Highest and Lowest call, is EL
// source and is exactly this loop: seed at the window's first bar and scan the
// rest with a strict comparison, so a tie keeps the earliest bar.
//
// THE TWO BENCHMARKS AT BAR kk BOTH EXCLUDE THE BREAKOUT BAR -- bars
// kk+1 .. kk+lookback -- so an oversized breakout bar cannot raise its own
// benchmark. THIS IS A STATED DEPARTURE from BreakoutBarRangeAndVolumeExpansion,
// whose TICK average is current-inclusive: that rule reads the tick count
// through an EL VARIABLE and averages the variable's zero-filled warm-up
// history, which cannot be re-read at a variable offset. Reading Ticks[kk + jj]
// directly is exact at every bar and needs no mirrored buffer (the `ticks` row
// is VERIFIED: up + down, not the up-ticks-only EL volume word), and it makes
// the two windows symmetric. The means are explicit loops for the same reason
// BodyExceedsAverageBarRange's is: Average over a series with real history is a
// fresh loop, but reading a function call's OWN history at an offset is
// unmeasured.
//
// THE REACTION BAR is BodyAboveAverageMultiple at consecutiveBars 1: a body of
// the right COLOUR, at least bodyMultiple times the mean body of the lookback
// bars before it. The mean BODY, not the mean RANGE -- that is the depends_on
// part, and BodyExceedsAverageBarRange is the harder mean-RANGE sibling used by
// the failure test instead.
//
// THREE For GUARDS. EasyLanguage's For counts DOWN when the end value is below
// the start, so `for jj = 1 to 0` would execute at jj = 1 and jj = 0 and read
// the CURRENT bar, and `for jj = kk + 2 to kk + 1` would read two bars of the
// level window backwards, where the C++ for-loops would not run at all. The
// guards make the twins agree at every setting; the grid still starts at 1 for
// lookback and maxBarsSinceBreakout and at 2 for breakoutLookback.
//
// SHIFT TEST: ranges, bodies and tick counts only.
//
// Deepest bar touched:
// max(maxBarsSinceBreakout + breakoutLookback, maxBarsSinceBreakout + lookback).
//
// Every scratch value is a localVariables entry rather than a hook-scope
// declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement; loop counters are
// block-scoped inside their loops. ctx.Ticks is the up-plus-down series, cast
// to double before arithmetic, never the `volume` alias.
bodySum = 0.0;
avgBody = 0.0;
for (int jj = 1; jj <= (int)lookback; ++jj) {
    bodySum = bodySum + std::fabs(close[jj] - open[jj]);
}
if ((int)lookback >= 1) {
    avgBody = bodySum / (int)lookback;
}
weakUp = false;
weakDn = false;
for (int kk = 1; kk <= (int)maxBarsSinceBreakout; ++kk) {
    if ((int)lookback < 1 || (int)breakoutLookback < 1) break;
    lvlHi = high[kk + 1];
    lvlLo = low[kk + 1];
    for (int jj = kk + 2; jj <= kk + (int)breakoutLookback; ++jj) {
        if (el_gt(high[jj], lvlHi)) lvlHi = high[jj];
        if (el_lt(low[jj], lvlLo)) lvlLo = low[jj];
    }
    rngSum = 0.0;
    volSum = 0.0;
    for (int jj = 1; jj <= (int)lookback; ++jj) {
        rngSum = rngSum + (high[kk + jj] - low[kk + jj]);
        volSum = volSum + (double)ctx.Ticks[kk + jj];
    }
    rngMean = rngSum / (int)lookback;
    volMean = volSum / (int)lookback;
    strongBrk = el_ge(high[kk] - low[kk], rangeMultiple * rngMean)
        && el_ge((double)ctx.Ticks[kk], volMultiple * volMean);
    if (el_gt(close[kk], lvlHi) && !strongBrk) weakUp = true;
    if (el_lt(close[kk], lvlLo) && !strongBrk) weakDn = true;
}
reactUp = el_gt(close[0], open[0]) && el_ge(close[0] - open[0], bodyMultiple * avgBody);
reactDn = el_lt(close[0], open[0]) && el_ge(open[0] - close[0], bodyMultiple * avgBody);
